<h1>undirected graph</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="graph.html"><em>graph</em></a> whose <a href="edge.html"><em>edges</em></a> are <em>unordered</em> pairs of  <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertices</em></a>.  That is, each edge connects two vertices.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Formal Definition:</strong> A graph G is a pair (V,E), where V is a set of vertices, and E is a set of edges between the vertices E &sube; {{u,v} | u, v &isin; V}. If the graph does not allow <a href="selfloop.html"><em>self-loops</em></a>, adjacency is <a href="irreflexive.html"><em>irreflexive</em></a>, that is E &sube; {{u,v} | u, v &isin; V &and; u &ne; v}. 
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="directedGraph.html"><em>directed graph</em></a>, <a href="hypergraph.html"><em>hypergraph</em></a>, <a href="multigraph.html"><em>multigraph</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
An undirected graph may be represented as a directed graph with two directed edges, one "to" and one "from," for each undirected edge.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

